const REFERENCE_HTML = `
<h2>Reference</h2>
<p class="sub">Values, sequences and rules the exam questions return to, grouped by week.</p>
<details open style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:10px;margin:12px 0;background:var(--paper)"><summary style="cursor:pointer;display:block;min-height:44px;box-sizing:border-box;padding:10px 12px;list-style-position:inside"><h3 id="ref-wk2" style="display:inline;margin:0;font-size:16px">Week 2: Intro to Dispensing (Dr. Lahoud)</h3></summary><div style="padding:0 12px 12px"><h4 id="ref-expiry" style="margin:14px 0 4px;font-size:14.5px">Label expiration dates</h4><ul style="margin:6px 0 6px 20px;padding:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5"><li style="margin:2px 0"><b>Compounded or reconstituted product:</b> check the product&rsquo;s own directions (Augmentin suspension: discard 10 days after mixing).</li><li style="margin:2px 0"><b>From a stock bottle:</b> the earlier of one year after the fill date and the stock bottle&rsquo;s expiration. A month-and-year stock date runs to the last day of that month.</li></ul><div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(230px,1fr));gap:10px;margin:10px 0"><div style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-left:5px solid #2f7fd0;border-radius:9px;padding:10px 12px;background:var(--paper);color:var(--text);font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.45;min-width:0;overflow-wrap:anywhere"><div style="font-weight:700;margin-bottom:4px">Filled 8/24/2026; stock bottle 2/2027</div><div style="margin:2px 0">One year after fill: 8/24/2027</div><div style="margin:2px 0">Stock expiration: last day of 2/2027 = 2/28/2027 (2027 is not a leap year)</div><div style="margin-top:5px;font-weight:700">Earlier date: label 02/28/2027</div></div><div style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-left:5px solid #2f7fd0;border-radius:9px;padding:10px 12px;background:var(--paper);color:var(--text);font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.45;min-width:0;overflow-wrap:anywhere"><div style="font-weight:700;margin-bottom:4px">Filled 3/1/2026; stock bottle 8/2027</div><div style="margin:2px 0">One year after fill: 3/1/2027</div><div style="margin:2px 0">Stock expiration: last day of 8/2027 = 8/31/2027</div><div style="margin-top:5px;font-weight:700">Earlier date: label 03/01/2027</div></div></div><h4 style="margin:14px 0 4px;font-size:14.5px">Three-step prescription review</h4><ol style="margin:6px 0 6px 22px;padding:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5"><li style="margin:3px 0"><b>Legal and safe?</b> Is the prescription legal and safe for the patient? A miss is a prescribing error: contact the prescriber.</li><li style="margin:3px 0"><b>Transcribed appropriately?</b> A miss is a transcribing error: correct the entry and regenerate labels.</li><li style="margin:3px 0"><b>Product matches?</b> Does the in-hand product match the transcribed product? A miss is a dispensing error: replace the product and/or relabel. Product verification checks medication, strength, formulation and expiration date.</li></ol><h4 style="margin:14px 0 4px;font-size:14.5px">Medication use process, in order</h4><ol style="margin:6px 0 6px 22px;padding:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5"><li style="margin:3px 0"><b>Prescribing</b></li><li style="margin:3px 0"><b>Order processing</b></li><li style="margin:3px 0"><b>Dispensing</b></li><li style="margin:3px 0"><b>Administration</b></li><li style="margin:3px 0"><b>Monitoring</b></li></ol><h4 style="margin:14px 0 4px;font-size:14.5px">Counseling: the three prime questions</h4><ol style="margin:6px 0 6px 22px;padding:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5"><li style="margin:3px 0"><b>What did the doctor tell you this medication was for?</b> (slide 43 says doctor; the rubric says provider)</li><li style="margin:3px 0"><b>How did the doctor tell you to take it?</b></li><li style="margin:3px 0"><b>What did the doctor tell you to expect?</b></li></ol><div style="font-weight:700;font-size:13px;margin:12px 0 2px;color:var(--text-dim)">Recall</div><details style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:9px;background:var(--paper);margin:6px 0"><summary style="cursor:pointer;display:block;min-height:40px;box-sizing:border-box;padding:10px 12px;font-weight:600;font-size:14px;line-height:20px;background:var(--surface);border-radius:9px">Filled 10/15/2026 from a stock bottle expiring 12/2026: label date?</summary><div style="padding:8px 12px 10px;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5">12/31/2026. The stock expiration is earlier than one year after fill, and a month-and-year date runs to the last day of the month.</div></details><details style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:9px;background:var(--paper);margin:6px 0"><summary style="cursor:pointer;display:block;min-height:40px;box-sizing:border-box;padding:10px 12px;font-weight:600;font-size:14px;line-height:20px;background:var(--surface);border-radius:9px">The three prime counseling questions?</summary><div style="padding:8px 12px 10px;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5">What the doctor said it was for; how the doctor said to take it; what the doctor said to expect.</div></details><p style="font-size:12px;color:var(--text-dim);margin:6px 0 16px">Source: Intro-to-Dispensing.pdf slides 6, 25&ndash;26, 36&ndash;39, 43; EOOR-Answer-Key.pdf station 10; 8/24 transcript for the prime questions and the Augmentin example.</p></div></details>
<details open style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:10px;margin:12px 0;background:var(--paper)"><summary style="cursor:pointer;display:block;min-height:44px;box-sizing:border-box;padding:10px 12px;list-style-position:inside"><h3 id="ref-wk3" style="display:inline;margin:0;font-size:16px">Week 3: OTC self-care (Dr. Parsons)</h3></summary><div style="padding:0 12px 12px"><h4 id="ref-assess" style="margin:14px 0 4px;font-size:14.5px">Pharmacist Patient Care Process (PPCP)</h4><ol style="margin:6px 0 6px 22px;padding:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5"><li style="margin:3px 0"><b>Collect</b></li><li style="margin:3px 0"><b>Assess</b></li><li style="margin:3px 0"><b>Plan</b></li><li style="margin:3px 0"><b>Implement</b></li><li style="margin:3px 0"><b>Follow-up</b></li></ol><p style="font-size:12.5px;color:var(--text-dim);margin:0 0 6px">Slide 4; developed by the Joint Commission of Pharmacy Practitioners.</p><h4 style="margin:14px 0 4px;font-size:14.5px">SCHOLAR-MAC, one letter at a time</h4><ol style="margin:6px 0 6px 22px;padding:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5"><li style="margin:3px 0"><b>Symptoms</b></li><li style="margin:3px 0"><b>Characteristics:</b> What are the symptoms like?</li><li style="margin:3px 0"><b>History:</b> What has been done so far? Has this happened in the past?</li><li style="margin:3px 0"><b>Onset:</b> When did it start?</li><li style="margin:3px 0"><b>Location:</b> Where is the problem?</li><li style="margin:3px 0"><b>Aggravating factors:</b> What makes it worse?</li><li style="margin:3px 0"><b>Remitting factors:</b> What makes it better?</li><li style="margin:3px 0"><b>Medications:</b> What prescription medications, OTC products, vitamins and supplements are you taking?</li><li style="margin:3px 0"><b>Allergies:</b> What allergies do you have?</li><li style="margin:3px 0"><b>Conditions:</b> What health conditions do you have, and are you pregnant or breastfeeding?</li></ol><h4 style="margin:14px 0 4px;font-size:14.5px">QuEST</h4><p style="margin:6px 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5">Quickly assess, Establish, Suggest, Talk: the full list is under Week 14.</p><h4 style="margin:14px 0 4px;font-size:14.5px">SOAP note</h4><ol style="margin:6px 0 6px 22px;padding:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5"><li style="margin:3px 0"><b>Subjective:</b> what the patient says (SCHOLAR-MAC)</li><li style="margin:3px 0"><b>Objective:</b> facts you and others observe: lab results, exam findings, procedure findings</li><li style="margin:3px 0"><b>Assessment:</b> your clinical interpretation, with rationale</li><li style="margin:3px 0"><b>Plan:</b> what you will do, follow-up, when to seek medical treatment, education</li></ol><h4 style="margin:14px 0 4px;font-size:14.5px">Exclusions and referrals</h4><div style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:9px;padding:8px 12px;margin:8px 0;background:var(--paper)"><div style="font-weight:700;font-size:13.5px;margin-bottom:2px">Refer to a medical provider (slide 14)</div><ul style="margin:6px 0 6px 20px;padding:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5"><li style="margin:2px 0">Tick bites</li><li style="margin:2px 0">Scabies</li><li style="margin:2px 0">Spider bites</li><li style="margin:2px 0">Secondary infection</li><li style="margin:2px 0">A bite or sting with systemic symptoms</li></ul></div><div style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:9px;padding:8px 12px;margin:8px 0;background:var(--paper)"><div style="font-weight:700;font-size:13.5px;margin-bottom:2px">Insect sting: not self-care (slides 21&ndash;22)</div><ul style="margin:6px 0 6px 20px;padding:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5"><li style="margin:2px 0">Hives, excessive swelling or dizziness</li><li style="margin:2px 0">Allergic response away from the sting: urgent care or the ER</li><li style="margin:2px 0">Severe reaction to previous insect bites or stings</li><li style="margin:2px 0">Personal or family history of significant allergic reactions, such as hay fever</li><li style="margin:2px 0">Age under 2 years</li></ul></div><div style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:9px;padding:8px 12px;margin:8px 0;background:var(--paper)"><div style="font-weight:700;font-size:13.5px;margin-bottom:2px">Head lice: not self-treatment (slides 31, 34)</div><ul style="margin:6px 0 6px 20px;padding:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5"><li style="margin:2px 0">Infestation of the eyelids or eyebrows</li><li style="margin:2px 0">Age under 2 months (permethrins) or under 2 years (pyrethrins)</li><li style="margin:2px 0">Secondary infection from scratching</li><li style="margin:2px 0">Pregnancy or breastfeeding: strong caution, refer to primary care</li><li style="margin:2px 0">Hypersensitivity to chrysanthemums, ragweed or pediculicide ingredients</li><li style="margin:2px 0">Active tumors; regional resistance</li></ul></div><div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(145px,1fr));gap:8px;margin:10px 0"><div style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:9px;padding:8px 10px;background:var(--paper);min-width:0;overflow-wrap:anywhere"><div style="font-size:19px;font-weight:700;line-height:1.2">7 days</div><div style="font-size:13px;margin-top:2px">Hydrocortisone 1%: up to 7 days</div><div style="font-size:11.5px;color:var(--text-dim);margin-top:3px">slide 27</div></div><div style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:9px;padding:8px 10px;background:var(--paper);min-width:0;overflow-wrap:anywhere"><div style="font-size:19px;font-weight:700;line-height:1.2">3&ndash;4&times; daily</div><div style="font-size:13px;margin-top:2px">Topical diphenhydramine, no longer than 7 days</div><div style="font-size:11.5px;color:var(--text-dim);margin-top:3px">slide 25</div></div><div style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:9px;padding:8px 10px;background:var(--paper);min-width:0;overflow-wrap:anywhere"><div style="font-size:19px;font-weight:700;line-height:1.2">10&ndash;15 min</div><div style="font-size:13px;margin-top:2px">Anaphylaxis can start this soon</div><div style="font-size:11.5px;color:var(--text-dim);margin-top:3px">slide 19</div></div><div style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:9px;padding:8px 10px;background:var(--paper);min-width:0;overflow-wrap:anywhere"><div style="font-size:19px;font-weight:700;line-height:1.2">6 hours</div><div style="font-size:13px;margin-top:2px">Anaphylaxis can appear up to this long after a sting</div><div style="font-size:11.5px;color:var(--text-dim);margin-top:3px">slide 19</div></div><div style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:9px;padding:8px 10px;background:var(--paper);min-width:0;overflow-wrap:anywhere"><div style="font-size:19px;font-weight:700;line-height:1.2">7&ndash;10 days</div><div style="font-size:13px;margin-top:2px">Lice retreatment window</div><div style="font-size:11.5px;color:var(--text-dim);margin-top:3px">slides 32&ndash;33</div></div><div style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:9px;padding:8px 10px;background:var(--paper);min-width:0;overflow-wrap:anywhere"><div style="font-size:19px;font-weight:700;line-height:1.2">10 minutes</div><div style="font-size:13px;margin-top:2px">Permethrin cream rinse on the scalp</div><div style="font-size:11.5px;color:var(--text-dim);margin-top:3px">slide 33</div></div></div><div style="font-weight:700;font-size:13px;margin:12px 0 2px;color:var(--text-dim)">Recall</div><details style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:9px;background:var(--paper);margin:6px 0"><summary style="cursor:pointer;display:block;min-height:40px;box-sizing:border-box;padding:10px 12px;font-weight:600;font-size:14px;line-height:20px;background:var(--surface);border-radius:9px">Which SCHOLAR-MAC elements ask what makes it worse and what makes it better?</summary><div style="padding:8px 12px 10px;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5">Aggravating factors: What makes it worse? Remitting factors: What makes it better?</div></details><details style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:9px;background:var(--paper);margin:6px 0"><summary style="cursor:pointer;display:block;min-height:40px;box-sizing:border-box;padding:10px 12px;font-weight:600;font-size:14px;line-height:20px;background:var(--surface);border-radius:9px">How long can topical antipruritics and anesthetics be used before a provider&rsquo;s advice is needed?</summary><div style="padding:8px 12px 10px;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5">7 days at most, unless a provider advises it (APhA excerpt).</div></details><p style="font-size:12px;color:var(--text-dim);margin:6px 0 16px">Source: OTC-Insect-Bites-Pediculosis.pdf slides 4, 6&ndash;10, 14, 19, 21&ndash;22, 25, 27, 31&ndash;34; APhA-Insect-Excerpt.pdf.</p></div></details>
<details open style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:10px;margin:12px 0;background:var(--paper)"><summary style="cursor:pointer;display:block;min-height:44px;box-sizing:border-box;padding:10px 12px;list-style-position:inside"><h3 id="ref-wk5" style="display:inline;margin:0;font-size:16px">Week 5: Point-of-care testing (Dr. Parsons)</h3></summary><div style="padding:0 12px 12px"><h4 id="ref-vitals" style="margin:14px 0 4px;font-size:14.5px">Vital signs and ranges</h4><div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(145px,1fr));gap:8px;margin:10px 0"><div style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:9px;padding:8px 10px;background:var(--paper);min-width:0;overflow-wrap:anywhere"><div style="font-size:19px;font-weight:700;line-height:1.2">60&ndash;100</div><div style="font-size:13px;margin-top:2px">Pulse, beats/min. Tachycardia above 100; bradycardia below 60</div><div style="font-size:11.5px;color:var(--text-dim);margin-top:3px">slides 30, 31</div></div><div style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:9px;padding:8px 10px;background:var(--paper);min-width:0;overflow-wrap:anywhere"><div style="font-size:19px;font-weight:700;line-height:1.2">12&ndash;20</div><div style="font-size:13px;margin-top:2px">Respirations, breaths/min. Tachypnea above 20; bradypnea below 12</div><div style="font-size:11.5px;color:var(--text-dim);margin-top:3px">slide 32</div></div><div style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:9px;padding:8px 10px;background:var(--paper);min-width:0;overflow-wrap:anywhere"><div style="font-size:19px;font-weight:700;line-height:1.2">below 120/80</div><div style="font-size:13px;margin-top:2px">Normal blood pressure (ACC/AHA 2017)</div><div style="font-size:11.5px;color:var(--text-dim);margin-top:3px">slide 38</div></div><div style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:9px;padding:8px 10px;background:var(--paper);min-width:0;overflow-wrap:anywhere"><div style="font-size:19px;font-weight:700;line-height:1.2">130/80 or more</div><div style="font-size:13px;margin-top:2px">Hypertension. Note: slide 38 prints Elevated as 120/80 or greater, which overlaps hypertension at 130/80; see the Study guide</div><div style="font-size:11.5px;color:var(--text-dim);margin-top:3px">slide 38</div></div><div style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:9px;padding:8px 10px;background:var(--paper);min-width:0;overflow-wrap:anywhere"><div style="font-size:19px;font-weight:700;line-height:1.2">180/110 or more</div><div style="font-size:13px;margin-top:2px">Hypertensive urgency</div><div style="font-size:11.5px;color:var(--text-dim);margin-top:3px">slide 38</div></div><div style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:9px;padding:8px 10px;background:var(--paper);min-width:0;overflow-wrap:anywhere"><div style="font-size:19px;font-weight:700;line-height:1.2">98.0&ndash;98.6 &deg;F</div><div style="font-size:13px;margin-top:2px">Normal temperature (36.7&ndash;37 &deg;C)</div><div style="font-size:11.5px;color:var(--text-dim);margin-top:3px">slide 39</div></div><div style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:9px;padding:8px 10px;background:var(--paper);min-width:0;overflow-wrap:anywhere"><div style="font-size:19px;font-weight:700;line-height:1.2">99.5 &deg;F or more</div><div style="font-size:13px;margin-top:2px">Fever (37.5 &deg;C); dangerous above 105.0 &deg;F</div><div style="font-size:11.5px;color:var(--text-dim);margin-top:3px">slide 39</div></div><div style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:9px;padding:8px 10px;background:var(--paper);min-width:0;overflow-wrap:anywhere"><div style="font-size:19px;font-weight:700;line-height:1.2">95&ndash;99%</div><div style="font-size:13px;margin-top:2px">Normal pulse oximetry</div><div style="font-size:11.5px;color:var(--text-dim);margin-top:3px">slide 40</div></div><div style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:9px;padding:8px 10px;background:var(--paper);min-width:0;overflow-wrap:anywhere"><div style="font-size:19px;font-weight:700;line-height:1.2">below 90%</div><div style="font-size:13px;margin-top:2px">Needs medical attention; can read falsely high in Black patients</div><div style="font-size:11.5px;color:var(--text-dim);margin-top:3px">slides 40, 41</div></div></div><p style="font-size:13px;margin:4px 0">Hypotension: systolic below 90&ndash;100 or diastolic below 50&ndash;60 (slide 38). Pulse count: 15 seconds &times; 4, or 30 seconds &times; 2. Respirations: do not tell the patient; at least 30 seconds (slide 32).</p><h4 style="margin:14px 0 4px;font-size:14.5px">Blood pressure technique</h4><ol style="margin:6px 0 6px 22px;padding:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5"><li style="margin:3px 0"><b>Position:</b> patient seated comfortably, arm at about heart level, palm up, feet flat on the floor, relaxed. The cuff may go over a shirt.</li><li style="margin:3px 0"><b>Inflate</b> rapidly to about 160&ndash;180 mmHg.</li><li style="margin:3px 0"><b>Deflate</b> at about 2&ndash;3 mmHg per second, with the stethoscope diaphragm over the brachial artery.</li><li style="margin:3px 0"><b>Repeat</b> if warranted: wait 5 minutes.</li></ol><h4 style="margin:14px 0 4px;font-size:14.5px">Swab technique</h4><ol style="margin:6px 0 6px 22px;padding:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5"><li style="margin:3px 0"><b>Throat:</b> start at the base of the arch at the level of the tonsil.</li><li style="margin:3px 0"><b>Follow</b> the glossopalatine arch and strike the uvula with the side of the swab.</li><li style="margin:3px 0"><b>End</b> at the opposite tonsil, then swab in reverse immediately.</li><li style="margin:3px 0"><b>Nostril:</b> rotate the swab 5 times with good contact and leave it in for 15 seconds; repeat in the other nostril if the manufacturer requires.</li></ol><p style="font-size:13px;margin:4px 0">A nasopharyngeal swab goes past the inferior meatus (slide 51). Not needed for exams: the oxygen saturation calculation (slide 40) and the Centor score (slide 58).</p><div style="font-weight:700;font-size:13px;margin:12px 0 2px;color:var(--text-dim)">Recall</div><details style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:9px;background:var(--paper);margin:6px 0"><summary style="cursor:pointer;display:block;min-height:40px;box-sizing:border-box;padding:10px 12px;font-weight:600;font-size:14px;line-height:20px;background:var(--surface);border-radius:9px">How is a pulse counted, and when is it repeated for a full minute?</summary><div style="padding:8px 12px 10px;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5">15 seconds &times; 4, or 30 seconds &times; 2. If the result is close to the tachycardia or bradycardia cut-off, repeat for the full minute.</div></details><details style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:9px;background:var(--paper);margin:6px 0"><summary style="cursor:pointer;display:block;min-height:40px;box-sizing:border-box;padding:10px 12px;font-weight:600;font-size:14px;line-height:20px;background:var(--surface);border-radius:9px">Blood pressure values for hypertension and hypertensive urgency?</summary><div style="padding:8px 12px 10px;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5">130/80 or greater for hypertension; 180/110 or greater for hypertensive urgency (ACC/AHA 2017).</div></details><p style="font-size:12px;color:var(--text-dim);margin:6px 0 16px">Source: POCT-Physical-Assessment.pdf slides 28&ndash;42, 46, 48&ndash;51, 58; TOSCE-Study-Guide.pdf page 1.</p></div></details>
<details open style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:10px;margin:12px 0;background:var(--paper)"><summary style="cursor:pointer;display:block;min-height:44px;box-sizing:border-box;padding:10px 12px;list-style-position:inside"><h3 id="ref-wk6" style="display:inline;margin:0;font-size:16px">Week 6: Immunizations (Dr. Parsons)</h3></summary><div style="padding:0 12px 12px"><h4 id="ref-aafp" style="margin:14px 0 4px;font-size:14.5px">Order of use of the AAFP adult schedule</h4><ol style="margin:6px 0 6px 22px;padding:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5"><li style="margin:3px 0"><b>Vaccines by age</b> (Table 1)</li><li style="margin:3px 0"><b>Medical conditions</b> (Table 2), often missed by providers</li><li style="margin:3px 0"><b>Notes</b>, which must be used on exams</li><li style="margin:3px 0"><b>Appendix:</b> contraindications and precautions</li><li style="margin:3px 0"><b>Addendum:</b> new or updated guidance</li></ol><!--DIA:tell-aafp--><h4 id="ref-aafp-color" style="margin:14px 0 4px;font-size:14.5px">The AAFP 2026 schedule in color</h4><p style="font-size:13.5px;margin:4px 0 8px">The colors carry the answer, and the two tables use different legends. Pinch to zoom on a phone. The Notes pages are not reproduced here.</p><div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(250px,1fr));gap:10px;margin:8px 0"><div style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-left:5px solid #2f7fd0;border-radius:9px;padding:8px 12px;background:var(--paper)"><b>Table 1 (by age)</b><ul style="margin:4px 0 0 18px;padding:0;font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.45"><li><b>Yellow:</b> recommended for adults who meet the age requirement and lack documentation or evidence of past infection</li><li><b>Light blue:</b> recommended for adults with an additional risk factor or another indication</li><li><b>Green:</b> recommended based on shared clinical decision-making</li><li><b>Gray:</b> no recommendation or not applicable</li></ul></div><div style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-left:5px solid #c9792a;border-radius:9px;padding:8px 12px;background:var(--paper)"><b>Table 2 (by condition)</b><ul style="margin:4px 0 0 18px;padding:0;font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.45"><li><b>Yellow:</b> recommended for all adults who lack documentation or evidence of past infection</li><li><b>Light green:</b> not recommended for all adults but recommended for some, based on age or increased risk</li><li><b>Teal:</b> recommended based on shared clinical decision-making</li><li><b>Orange:</b> recommended for all adults, and additional doses may be necessary based on condition</li><li><b>Navy:</b> precaution, might be indicated if the benefit outweighs the risk of an adverse reaction</li><li><b>Red:</b> contraindicated or not recommended (*vaccinate after pregnancy if indicated)</li><li><b>Gray:</b> no guidance or not applicable</li></ul></div></div>{{fig:aafp_t1|Table 1: recommended adult immunization schedule by age group, 2026 (AAFP)}}{{fig:aafp_t2|Table 2: recommended schedule by medical condition or other indication, 2026 (AAFP)}}{{fig:aafp_app1|Appendix, page 1: contraindications and precautions for COVID-19 and influenza vaccines}}{{fig:aafp_app2|Appendix, page 2: contraindications and precautions for the other vaccines}}<p style="font-size:11.5px;color:var(--text-dim);margin:2px 0 8px">Source: AAFP-Adult-Schedule-2026.pdf, Tables 1 and 2 and Appendix (pages 2, 3, 9 and 10).</p><h4 style="margin:14px 0 4px;font-size:14.5px">Intervals and regimens</h4><div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(145px,1fr));gap:8px;margin:10px 0"><div style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:9px;padding:8px 10px;background:var(--paper);min-width:0;overflow-wrap:anywhere"><div style="font-size:19px;font-weight:700;line-height:1.2">28 days</div><div style="font-size:13px;margin-top:2px">4 weeks = 28 days</div><div style="font-size:11.5px;color:var(--text-dim);margin-top:3px">AAFP Notes (additional information)</div></div><div style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:9px;padding:8px 10px;background:var(--paper);min-width:0;overflow-wrap:anywhere"><div style="font-size:19px;font-weight:700;line-height:1.2">4 days early</div><div style="font-size:13px;margin-top:2px">A dose up to 4 days before the minimum age or interval is valid</div><div style="font-size:11.5px;color:var(--text-dim);margin-top:3px">AAFP Notes (additional information)</div></div><div style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:9px;padding:8px 10px;background:var(--paper);min-width:0;overflow-wrap:anywhere"><div style="font-size:19px;font-weight:700;line-height:1.2">5+ days early</div><div style="font-size:13px;margin-top:2px">Repeat the dose</div><div style="font-size:11.5px;color:var(--text-dim);margin-top:3px">AAFP Notes (additional information)</div></div><div style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:9px;padding:8px 10px;background:var(--paper);min-width:0;overflow-wrap:anywhere"><div style="font-size:19px;font-weight:700;line-height:1.2">2&ndash;6 months</div><div style="font-size:13px;margin-top:2px">RZV dose 2 after dose 1; minimum interval 4 weeks</div><div style="font-size:11.5px;color:var(--text-dim);margin-top:3px">Slide 22; AAFP Notes</div></div><div style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:9px;padding:8px 10px;background:var(--paper);min-width:0;overflow-wrap:anywhere"><div style="font-size:19px;font-weight:700;line-height:1.2">28 days apart</div><div style="font-size:13px;margin-top:2px">Mpox (Jynneos): 2 doses</div><div style="font-size:11.5px;color:var(--text-dim);margin-top:3px">AAFP Notes</div></div><div style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:9px;padding:8px 10px;background:var(--paper);min-width:0;overflow-wrap:anywhere"><div style="font-size:19px;font-weight:700;line-height:1.2">0, 1, 6 months</div><div style="font-size:13px;margin-top:2px">Hepatitis B, dialysis: Recombivax HB, 3 doses (dialysis formulation 1 mL = 40 mcg)</div><div style="font-size:11.5px;color:var(--text-dim);margin-top:3px">AAFP Notes</div></div><div style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:9px;padding:8px 10px;background:var(--paper);min-width:0;overflow-wrap:anywhere"><div style="font-size:19px;font-weight:700;line-height:1.2">0, 1, 2, 6 months</div><div style="font-size:13px;margin-top:2px">Hepatitis B, dialysis: Engerix-B, 4 doses, 2 mL dose</div><div style="font-size:11.5px;color:var(--text-dim);margin-top:3px">AAFP Notes</div></div><div style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:9px;padding:8px 10px;background:var(--paper);min-width:0;overflow-wrap:anywhere"><div style="font-size:19px;font-weight:700;line-height:1.2">every 10 years</div><div style="font-size:13px;margin-top:2px">Td or Tdap after the primary series and first Tdap</div><div style="font-size:11.5px;color:var(--text-dim);margin-top:3px">AAFP Notes</div></div></div><div style="font-weight:700;font-size:13px;margin:12px 0 2px;color:var(--text-dim)">Recall</div><details style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:9px;background:var(--paper);margin:6px 0"><summary style="cursor:pointer;display:block;min-height:40px;box-sizing:border-box;padding:10px 12px;font-weight:600;font-size:14px;line-height:20px;background:var(--surface);border-radius:9px">A dose is given 4 days before the minimum interval. Valid?</summary><div style="padding:8px 12px 10px;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5">Valid. A dose given 5 or more days early is repeated. 4 weeks equals 28 days.</div></details><details style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:9px;background:var(--paper);margin:6px 0"><summary style="cursor:pointer;display:block;min-height:40px;box-sizing:border-box;padding:10px 12px;font-weight:600;font-size:14px;line-height:20px;background:var(--surface);border-radius:9px">Hepatitis B on dialysis: Recombivax HB versus Engerix-B?</summary><div style="padding:8px 12px 10px;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5">Recombivax HB (dialysis formulation): 3 doses at 0, 1 and 6 months. Engerix-B: 4 doses at 0, 1, 2 and 6 months, with a 2 mL dose.</div></details><p style="font-size:12px;color:var(--text-dim);margin:6px 0 16px">Source: Immunizations-Pre-Lab.pdf slides 8, 22; AAFP-Adult-Schedule-2026.pdf Notes and Appendix.</p></div></details>
<details open style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:10px;margin:12px 0;background:var(--paper)"><summary style="cursor:pointer;display:block;min-height:44px;box-sizing:border-box;padding:10px 12px;list-style-position:inside"><h3 id="ref-wk14" style="display:inline;margin:0;font-size:16px">Week 14: DEENT lab (Dr. Lahoud)</h3></summary><div style="padding:0 12px 12px"><h4 style="margin:14px 0 4px;font-size:14.5px">QuEST</h4><ol style="margin:6px 0 6px 22px;padding:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5"><li style="margin:3px 0"><b>Quickly assess</b> with SCHOLAR-MAC</li><li style="margin:3px 0"><b>Establish</b> that the patient is a self-care candidate</li><li style="margin:3px 0"><b>Suggest</b> self-care strategies</li><li style="margin:3px 0"><b>Talk</b> about the selected product(s): administration, efficacy (what to expect), safety (adverse effects)</li></ol><h4 style="margin:14px 0 4px;font-size:14.5px">Which-is-which numbers</h4><div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(145px,1fr));gap:8px;margin:10px 0"><div style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:9px;padding:8px 10px;background:var(--paper);min-width:0;overflow-wrap:anywhere"><div style="font-size:19px;font-weight:700;line-height:1.2">twice a day</div><div style="font-size:13px;margin-top:2px">Ketotifen, patients 3 years and older (allergic conjunctivitis)</div><div style="font-size:11.5px;color:var(--text-dim);margin-top:3px">Ophthalmic-Review-Key.pdf</div></div><div style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:9px;padding:8px 10px;background:var(--paper);min-width:0;overflow-wrap:anywhere"><div style="font-size:19px;font-weight:700;line-height:1.2">once or twice a day</div><div style="font-size:13px;margin-top:2px">Artificial tears, on arising and before retiring (dry eye)</div><div style="font-size:11.5px;color:var(--text-dim);margin-top:3px">Ophthalmic-Review-Key.pdf</div></div><div style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:9px;padding:8px 10px;background:var(--paper);min-width:0;overflow-wrap:anywhere"><div style="font-size:19px;font-weight:700;line-height:1.2">q2h &times; 2 days, then q4h &times; 5 days</div><div style="font-size:13px;margin-top:2px">Ciprofloxacin ophthalmic solution, 1&ndash;2 drops (bacterial or viral conjunctivitis)</div><div style="font-size:11.5px;color:var(--text-dim);margin-top:3px">Ophthalmic-Review-Key.pdf</div></div><div style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:9px;padding:8px 10px;background:var(--paper);min-width:0;overflow-wrap:anywhere"><div style="font-size:19px;font-weight:700;line-height:1.2">once daily</div><div style="font-size:13px;margin-top:2px">Latanoprost (POAG); refrigerate before opening</div><div style="font-size:11.5px;color:var(--text-dim);margin-top:3px">Ophthalmic-Review-Key.pdf</div></div><div style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:9px;padding:8px 10px;background:var(--paper);min-width:0;overflow-wrap:anywhere"><div style="font-size:19px;font-weight:700;line-height:1.2">5 minutes</div><div style="font-size:13px;margin-top:2px">Between two eye solutions</div><div style="font-size:11.5px;color:var(--text-dim);margin-top:3px">Lam_Glaucoma.pdf slide 74</div></div><div style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:9px;padding:8px 10px;background:var(--paper);min-width:0;overflow-wrap:anywhere"><div style="font-size:19px;font-weight:700;line-height:1.2">15 minutes</div><div style="font-size:13px;margin-top:2px">Wait after instilling drops before reinserting contact lenses</div><div style="font-size:11.5px;color:var(--text-dim);margin-top:3px">Lam_Glaucoma.pdf slide 83</div></div><div style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:9px;padding:8px 10px;background:var(--paper);min-width:0;overflow-wrap:anywhere"><div style="font-size:19px;font-weight:700;line-height:1.2">72 hours</div><div style="font-size:13px;margin-top:2px">OTC regimen used correctly without resolution: patient needs a provider</div><div style="font-size:11.5px;color:var(--text-dim);margin-top:3px">Lam_OphthalmicDisorders.pdf slides 39, 41</div></div><div style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:9px;padding:8px 10px;background:var(--paper);min-width:0;overflow-wrap:anywhere"><div style="font-size:19px;font-weight:700;line-height:1.2">8 weeks or more</div><div style="font-size:13px;margin-top:2px">Time to visible acne benefit; 10&ndash;25% lesion decrease within 4&ndash;8 weeks</div><div style="font-size:11.5px;color:var(--text-dim);margin-top:3px">Patel slides 41, 44</div></div></div><h4 style="margin:14px 0 4px;font-size:14.5px">Eye drops: order of application</h4><ol style="margin:6px 0 6px 22px;padding:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5"><li style="margin:3px 0"><b>Solution</b></li><li style="margin:3px 0"><b>Suspension</b></li><li style="margin:3px 0"><b>Ointment or gel-forming solution</b>, last</li></ol><h4 style="margin:14px 0 4px;font-size:14.5px">Glaucoma drug classes, in the order added</h4><ol style="margin:6px 0 6px 22px;padding:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5"><li style="margin:3px 0"><b>Prostaglandin analog</b> (starts therapy)</li><li style="margin:3px 0"><b>Beta blocker</b></li><li style="margin:3px 0"><b>Alpha-2 agonist</b></li><li style="margin:3px 0"><b>Topical carbonic anhydrase inhibitor</b></li><li style="margin:3px 0"><b>Cholinergic</b></li><li style="margin:3px 0"><b>Rho kinase inhibitor</b></li></ol><h4 style="margin:14px 0 4px;font-size:14.5px">Exclusions</h4><div style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:9px;padding:8px 12px;margin:8px 0;background:var(--paper)"><div style="font-weight:700;font-size:13.5px;margin-bottom:2px">Eye: not self-care (dry eye and conjunctivitis, slides 11, 30)</div><ul style="margin:6px 0 6px 20px;padding:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5"><li style="margin:2px 0">Eye pain</li><li style="margin:2px 0">Ocular trauma or chemical exposure</li><li style="margin:2px 0">Eye exposure to heat (not sun exposure)</li><li style="margin:2px 0">Blurred vision not due to eye ointments</li><li style="margin:2px 0">Light sensitivity</li><li style="margin:2px 0">Signs of eyelid infection other than a stye</li></ul></div><div style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:9px;padding:8px 12px;margin:8px 0;background:var(--paper)"><div style="font-weight:700;font-size:13.5px;margin-bottom:2px">Acne: not self-care (slide 18)</div><ul style="margin:6px 0 6px 20px;padding:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5"><li style="margin:2px 0">Severe acne</li><li style="margin:2px 0">Possible rosacea</li><li style="margin:2px 0">Exacerbating factors</li><li style="margin:2px 0">Comedogenic drugs</li></ul></div><div style="font-weight:700;font-size:13px;margin:12px 0 2px;color:var(--text-dim)">Recall</div><details style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:9px;background:var(--paper);margin:6px 0"><summary style="cursor:pointer;display:block;min-height:40px;box-sizing:border-box;padding:10px 12px;font-weight:600;font-size:14px;line-height:20px;background:var(--surface);border-radius:9px">How long between two eye solutions, and which goes last?</summary><div style="padding:8px 12px 10px;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5">5 minutes between two solutions. An ointment or gel-forming solution goes last (solution, suspension, then ointment or gel).</div></details><details style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:9px;background:var(--paper);margin:6px 0"><summary style="cursor:pointer;display:block;min-height:40px;box-sizing:border-box;padding:10px 12px;font-weight:600;font-size:14px;line-height:20px;background:var(--surface);border-radius:9px">What do the letters of QuEST stand for?</summary><div style="padding:8px 12px 10px;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5">Quickly assess (SCHOLAR-MAC); Establish self-care candidacy; Suggest self-care strategies; Talk about the product.</div></details><p style="font-size:12px;color:var(--text-dim);margin:6px 0 16px">Source: DEENT-Lab-Deck.pdf; Ophthalmic-Review-Key.pdf; Lam_OphthalmicDisorders.pdf slides 11, 30, 39, 41; Lam_Glaucoma.pdf slides 19, 63, 74, 83; Patel_Acne-Therapeutics-Student.pdf slides 18, 41, 44 as cited in the DEENT bank.</p></div></details>
<details style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:10px;margin:12px 0;background:var(--paper)"><summary style="cursor:pointer;display:block;min-height:44px;box-sizing:border-box;padding:10px 12px;list-style-position:inside"><h3 id="ref-deent" style="display:inline;margin:0;font-size:16px">DEENT lecture slides to review (Dr. Lahoud lab)</h3></summary><div style="padding:0 12px 12px"><p style="font-size:13.5px;margin:4px 0 8px;color:var(--text)">Slides from the DEENT lab lectures. Pinch to zoom on a phone. Each note says what to read from the picture.</p><h4 style="margin:14px 0 4px;font-size:14.5px">Dr. Patel: acne</h4><div style="font-weight:700;font-size:14px;margin:14px 0 2px">Acne classification, types 3 and 4 (slide 8)</div>{{fig:deent_acne_sev|Acne types 3 (moderate) and 4 (severe): lesions, nodules and scarring}}<ul style="margin:4px 0 8px 20px;padding:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5"><li style="margin:3px 0">Moderate: many noninflammatory lesions, some inflammatory lesions, no more than one nodule, mild scarring possible.</li><li style="margin:3px 0">Severe: many noninflammatory and inflammatory lesions, no more than a few nodular lesions, moderate to extensive scarring.</li><li style="margin:3px 0">The two rows differ on inflammatory lesion count, nodules and scarring.</li></ul><div style="font-weight:700;font-size:14px;margin:14px 0 2px">Non-prescription topical products (slide 23)</div>{{fig:deent_acne_otc|OTC acne products compared: bactericidal, keratolytic, comedolytic, strength, form, adverse effects}}<ul style="margin:4px 0 8px 20px;padding:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5"><li style="margin:3px 0">Only benzoyl peroxide is both bactericidal and comedolytic; resorcinol is bactericidal and antifungal but not comedolytic.</li><li style="margin:3px 0">Retinoid and hydroxy acids are not bactericidal; hydroxy acids are comedolytic as the beta form.</li><li style="margin:3px 0">Read the adverse effects row: benzoyl peroxide bleaches hair and clothing; hydroxy acids increase photosensitivity; sulfur has color and odor.</li></ul><div style="font-weight:700;font-size:14px;margin:14px 0 2px">Topical formulation selection (slide 24)</div>{{fig:deent_acne_form|Vehicle by skin type: gels, solutions, lotions and creams}}<ul style="margin:4px 0 8px 20px;padding:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5"><li style="margin:3px 0">Oily to normal skin tolerates gels, solutions and lotions.</li><li style="margin:3px 0">Normal to dry or &ldquo;sensitive&rdquo; skin tolerates lotions and creams.</li><li style="margin:3px 0">Ointments and bars are not usually recommended for any skin type.</li></ul><div style="font-weight:700;font-size:14px;margin:14px 0 2px">Prescription topical agents (slide 31)</div>{{fig:deent_acne_rx|Prescription topical acne agents: class, products, effects, adverse effects, comments}}<ul style="margin:4px 0 8px 20px;padding:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5"><li style="margin:3px 0">Topical antibiotics (clindamycin, erythromycin, minocycline foam) are not used as monotherapy; add benzoyl peroxide to reduce resistance.</li><li style="margin:3px 0">Wait 30 minutes to shave or apply cosmetics after a topical antibiotic.</li><li style="margin:3px 0">Dapsone and azelaic acid sit in the anti-inflammatory row; the comments column carries a G6PD concern with monitoring of CBC and reticulocyte counts.</li></ul><p style="font-size:11.5px;color:var(--text-dim);margin:2px 0 8px">Source: Patel_Acne-Therapeutics-Student.pdf, slides 8, 23, 24 and 31.</p><h4 style="margin:14px 0 4px;font-size:14.5px">Dr. Patel: scaly dermatoses</h4><div style="font-weight:700;font-size:14px;margin:14px 0 2px">Dandruff, seborrheic dermatitis and psoriasis compared (slide 6)</div>{{fig:deent_scaly_compare|Location, exacerbating factors, appearance, inflammation, hyperplasia and epidermal kinetics for the three conditions}}<ul style="margin:4px 0 8px 20px;padding:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5"><li style="margin:3px 0">Inflammation and epidermal hyperplasia are absent in dandruff and present in seborrheic dermatitis and psoriasis.</li><li style="margin:3px 0">Seborrheic dermatitis: yellow, oily scales on red skin. Psoriasis: sharp-bordered red plaques with silvery white scales.</li><li style="margin:3px 0">Epidermal turnover is 2 times, 3 times and 8 times faster than normal in dandruff, seborrheic dermatitis and psoriasis.</li></ul><div style="font-weight:700;font-size:14px;margin:14px 0 2px">FDA approved OTC ingredients (slide 7)</div>{{fig:deent_scaly_otc|OTC ingredient strengths for dandruff, seborrheic dermatitis and psoriasis}}<ul style="margin:4px 0 8px 20px;padding:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5"><li style="margin:3px 0">Hydrocortisone 1% reads &ldquo;no&rdquo; for dandruff; sulfur reads &ldquo;no&rdquo; for psoriasis.</li><li style="margin:3px 0">Pyrithione zinc strength differs by condition and by rinse-off or residual product.</li><li style="margin:3px 0">Coal tar, ketoconazole shampoo, salicylic acid and selenium sulfide are marked for all three conditions.</li></ul><div style="font-weight:700;font-size:14px;margin:14px 0 2px">Body surface area (slide 32)</div>{{fig:deent_psor_bsa|Body surface area percentages for an adult and a child}}<ul style="margin:4px 0 8px 20px;padding:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5"><li style="margin:3px 0">Adult: head 9%, chest 18%, back 18%, each arm 9%, each leg 18%, perineum 1%.</li><li style="margin:3px 0">Child: head 18%, chest 18%, back 18%, each arm 9%, each leg 13.5%, perineum 1%.</li><li style="margin:3px 0">The head and the legs are the regions that change between adult and child.</li></ul><div style="font-weight:700;font-size:14px;margin:14px 0 2px">Treatment algorithm for mild-to-moderate psoriasis (slide 35)</div>{{fig:deent_psor_algo|Algorithm: topical agents, then topical agents plus phototherapy, then topical agents plus a systemic agent}}<ul style="margin:4px 0 8px 20px;padding:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5"><li style="margin:3px 0">Each step is taken only if the step before is inadequate or ineffective.</li><li style="margin:3px 0">Moisturizers are used as needed; when disease is controlled, step down to the lowest doses or potencies that keep control.</li></ul><div style="font-weight:700;font-size:14px;margin:14px 0 2px">Psoriasis: topical therapy options (slide 36)</div>{{fig:deent_psor_topical|Topical psoriasis products and how each is applied}}<ul style="margin:4px 0 8px 20px;padding:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5"><li style="margin:3px 0">Corticosteroid: 2 to 4 times daily, cream or lotion in the day and ointment at night; low potency for face and skin folds; limit to 4 weeks or less.</li><li style="margin:3px 0">Anthralin goes only on thick plaques for 2 hours or less, then is wiped off; zinc oxide protects normal skin at the edges.</li><li style="margin:3px 0">Coal tar goes on in the evening and is washed off in the morning; pimecrolimus is for intertriginous areas.</li></ul><p style="font-size:11.5px;color:var(--text-dim);margin:2px 0 8px">Source: Patel_Scaly-Dermatoses-Therapeutics-Student.pdf, slides 6, 7, 32, 35 and 36.</p><h4 style="margin:14px 0 4px;font-size:14.5px">Dr. Lam: ophthalmic disorders</h4><div style="font-weight:700;font-size:14px;margin:14px 0 2px">Exclusions for self-treatment (slide 11)</div>{{fig:deent_oph_excl|Findings that exclude self-treatment of eye complaints}}<ul style="margin:4px 0 8px 20px;padding:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5"><li style="margin:3px 0">Eye pain, ocular trauma, chemical exposure, heat exposure to the eye (not sun exposure) and light sensitivity.</li><li style="margin:3px 0">Blurred vision counts only when it is not caused by an eye ointment.</li><li style="margin:3px 0">Signs of eyelid infection (red, thickened eyelids, scaling) exclude self-treatment, except a stye.</li></ul><div style="font-weight:700;font-size:14px;margin:14px 0 2px">Bacterial and viral conjunctivitis (slide 28)</div>{{fig:deent_conj_bv|Etiology, onset, symptoms and treatment for bacterial and viral conjunctivitis}}<ul style="margin:4px 0 8px 20px;padding:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5"><li style="margin:3px 0">Bacterial: acute (tearing, lid crusting) or chronic (lid crusting). Viral: adenoviral (tearing, lid crusting on waking) or herpetic (tearing).</li><li style="margin:3px 0">Treatment column for each row: symptomatic treatment and refer to a provider.</li><li style="margin:3px 0">The Condition column is marked FYI, and the last row is cut off at the bottom of the slide.</li></ul><div style="font-weight:700;font-size:14px;margin:14px 0 2px">Allergic conjunctivitis (slide 29)</div>{{fig:deent_conj_allergic|Seasonal, vernal and giant papillary allergic conjunctivitis}}<ul style="margin:4px 0 8px 20px;padding:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5"><li style="margin:3px 0">Itching appears in every row and is underlined on the slide.</li><li style="margin:3px 0">Vernal and giant papillary add mucous discharge; giant papillary adds contact lens intolerance and an acute onset.</li><li style="margin:3px 0">Treatment is symptomatic relief with the agents on the later slides.</li></ul><p style="font-size:11.5px;color:var(--text-dim);margin:2px 0 8px">Source: Lam_OphthalmicDisorders.pdf, slides 11, 28 and 29.</p><h4 style="margin:14px 0 4px;font-size:14.5px">Dr. Lam: glaucoma</h4><div style="font-weight:700;font-size:14px;margin:14px 0 2px">Beta blocker drug names and dosing (slide 32)</div>{{fig:deent_glc_names|Timolol, betaxolol (printed as bextaxolol on the slide), carteolol and levobunolol products with dosing}}<ul style="margin:4px 0 8px 20px;padding:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5"><li style="margin:3px 0">Timolol solution is 1 drop twice daily; Timoptic XE gel is 1 drop daily.</li><li style="margin:3px 0">Bextaxolol (Betoptic), carteolol (Ocupress) and levobunolol (Betagan) are each 1 drop twice daily.</li><li style="margin:3px 0">Timolol also appears in two combinations on this slide: with dorzolamide (Cosopt) and with brimonidine (Combigan).</li></ul><div style="font-weight:700;font-size:14px;margin:14px 0 2px">Combination eye prescriptions (slide 69)</div>{{fig:deent_glc_combo|Combination glaucoma drops: generic name, class and dosing regimen}}<ul style="margin:4px 0 8px 20px;padding:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5"><li style="margin:3px 0">Combigan (brimonidine/timolol) is alpha-2 agonist plus beta blocker, 1 drop twice daily.</li><li style="margin:3px 0">Cosopt and Cosopt PF (dorzolamide/timolol) are CAI plus beta blocker, 1 drop twice daily.</li><li style="margin:3px 0">Simbrinza (brinzolamide/brimonidine) is CAI plus alpha-2 agonist, 1 drop three times daily.</li></ul><div style="font-weight:700;font-size:14px;margin:14px 0 2px">Eyelid closure (slide 72)</div>{{fig:deent_glc_elc|Eyelid closure and the lacrimal drainage path}}<ul style="margin:4px 0 8px 20px;padding:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5"><li style="margin:3px 0">Eyelid closure reduces nasolacrimal drainage, systemic absorption and the severity of systemic side effects.</li><li style="margin:3px 0">The labeled path runs from the punctum and canaliculus through the lacrimal sac and nasolacrimal duct to the nasal cavity.</li></ul><div style="font-weight:700;font-size:14px;margin:14px 0 2px">Drugs that worsen angle-closure glaucoma (slide 90)</div>{{fig:deent_glc_pacg|Drug classes that worsen or exacerbate primary angle-closure glaucoma}}<ul style="margin:4px 0 8px 20px;padding:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5"><li style="margin:3px 0">Topical and systemic anticholinergics, antidepressants, alpha-1 adrenergic agonists and beta-2 adrenergic agonists.</li><li style="margin:3px 0">The slide is about angle-closure glaucoma, not primary open-angle glaucoma.</li></ul><p style="font-size:11.5px;color:var(--text-dim);margin:2px 0 8px">Source: Lam_Glaucoma.pdf, slides 32, 69, 72 and 90.</p></div></details>
<details style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:10px;margin:12px 0;background:var(--paper)"><summary style="cursor:pointer;display:block;min-height:44px;box-sizing:border-box;padding:10px 12px;list-style-position:inside"><h3 id="ref-sbar" style="display:inline;margin:0;font-size:16px">SBAR practice: builder, case drills, immunization case</h3></summary><div style="padding:0 12px 12px"><h4 style="margin:18px 0 6px;font-size:1.05em;color:var(--text)">1. SBAR builder for a prescription error</h4>
<p style="margin:6px 0;color:var(--text)">Use this version when you call a prescriber about a prescribing error. Write the four parts in this order and say them in this order.</p>
<ol style="margin:6px 0 6px 20px;padding:0;color:var(--text)"><li style="margin:6px 0"><b>Situation:</b> What is the reason for your call?</li><li style="margin:6px 0"><b>Background:</b> Who is the patient? What is the prescription in question? What are the clinical facts surrounding the problem?</li><li style="margin:6px 0"><b>Assessment:</b> Based on the information in the background, what is the actual problem and why does it matter?</li><li style="margin:6px 0"><b>Recommendation:</b> What should be done to correct the problem?</li></ol>
<div style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:8px;background:var(--paper);color:var(--text);padding:10px 12px;margin:10px 0;box-sizing:border-box;max-width:100%;overflow-wrap:anywhere"><b>Background versus Assessment</b><ul style="margin:6px 0 6px 18px;padding:0;color:var(--text)"><li style="margin:3px 0">Background = the clinical facts needed to understand the situation.</li><li style="margin:3px 0">Assessment = what the actual problem is.</li><li style="margin:3px 0">Example: Background: the maximum prescribed dose of ibuprofen is 3.2 g/day. Assessment: the total daily dose prescribed is too high.</li><li style="margin:3px 0">Example: Background: the maximum frequency of the drug is once daily. Assessment: the frequency prescribed is too high.</li></ul></div>
<div style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:8px;background:var(--paper);color:var(--text);padding:10px 12px;margin:10px 0;box-sizing:border-box;max-width:100%;overflow-wrap:anywhere"><b>Which error needs this call</b><ul style="margin:6px 0 6px 18px;padding:0;color:var(--text)"><li style="margin:3px 0"><b>Prescribing error</b>: the prescription itself is wrong. Contact the prescriber to clarify, correct or obtain a new prescription. This is the error that needs an SBAR to the provider.</li><li style="margin:3px 0"><b>Transcribing error</b>: the pharmacy entry or label differs from the prescription. Correct the entry in the pharmacy system and regenerate the labels. No SBAR to the prescriber.</li><li style="margin:3px 0"><b>Dispensing error</b>: the wrong product, strength, formulation or quantity was filled, or the use-before date or product expiration is wrong. Replace the product or relabel before dispensing. No SBAR to the prescriber.</li></ul></div>
<ul style="margin:6px 0 6px 18px;padding:0;color:var(--text)"><li style="margin:3px 0">Write an SBAR outline before you speak, and say the parts in logical order.</li><li style="margin:3px 0">Check Lexidrug closely to identify the error.</li><li style="margin:3px 0">Be complete but concise. If you have trouble forming the SBAR, think in reverse from the recommendation; that is a way to draft it, not the order to speak it.</li></ul>
<p style="margin:6px 0 14px;font-size:0.82em;color:var(--text-dim)">Source: APC-III-Week-11-OSCE-Prep_2026.pdf (SBAR for EOOR; SBAR review: components; Background vs Assessment; Error for SBAR); Intro-to-Dispensing.pdf slides 38–39; Immunizations-Pre-Lab.pdf slide 20.</p>
<h4 style="margin:18px 0 6px;font-size:1.05em;color:var(--text)">Builder: one worked mini-SBAR for each prescribing error type</h4>
<p style="margin:6px 0;color:var(--text)">Seven prescribing errors are on the Week 11 list. For each card, write the four parts yourself first, then open the model.</p>
<div style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:8px;background:var(--paper);color:var(--text);padding:10px 12px;margin:10px 0;box-sizing:border-box;max-width:100%;overflow-wrap:anywhere"><b>1. Prescription is expired</b><p style="margin:4px 0;color:var(--text)"><i>Case:</i> Clonazepam 0.5 mg written 4/6/2026, presented 11/21/2026.</p><details style="border-top:1px solid var(--line);margin:6px 0 0;padding:0"><summary style="min-height:40px;display:flex;align-items:center;cursor:pointer;font-weight:600;padding:4px 0;box-sizing:border-box">Try it: write the four parts, then open this</summary><div style="padding:4px 0 8px"><ul style="list-style:none;margin:4px 0;padding:0;color:var(--text)"><li style="margin:4px 0"><b>S:</b> I am calling about a clonazepam prescription for your patient.</li><li style="margin:4px 0"><b>B:</b> The patient is 46 years old; the prescription for clonazepam 0.5 mg was written on 4/6/2026, and a CIII–V prescription expires 6 months after it is issued.</li><li style="margin:4px 0"><b>A:</b> Six months after 4/6/2026 is 10/6/2026, and 11/21/2026 is 46 days past that date, so the prescription has expired and cannot be filled.</li><li style="margin:4px 0"><b>R:</b> I recommend that you send a new prescription for clonazepam 0.5 mg.</li></ul><p style="margin:6px 0;font-size:0.85em;color:var(--text-dim)">Basis: Schedule III–V prescriptions expire 6 months after issue; dangerous drugs 1 year; schedule II 30 days (Intro to Dispensing slide 22). EOOR key, station 6: contact prescriber for a new prescription. Clonazepam is CS-IV in the Basic Drug Knowledge list. The 0.5 mg strength and the age are invented for practice.</p></div></details></div>
<div style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:8px;background:var(--paper);color:var(--text);padding:10px 12px;margin:10px 0;box-sizing:border-box;max-width:100%;overflow-wrap:anywhere"><b>2. Prescribed dose is too high or too low</b><p style="margin:4px 0;color:var(--text)"><i>Case:</i> Ibuprofen 800 mg every 4 hours as needed.</p><details style="border-top:1px solid var(--line);margin:6px 0 0;padding:0"><summary style="min-height:40px;display:flex;align-items:center;cursor:pointer;font-weight:600;padding:4px 0;box-sizing:border-box">Try it: write the four parts, then open this</summary><div style="padding:4px 0 8px"><ul style="list-style:none;margin:4px 0;padding:0;color:var(--text)"><li style="margin:4px 0"><b>S:</b> I am calling about an ibuprofen prescription you sent to our pharmacy today.</li><li style="margin:4px 0"><b>B:</b> The patient is a 46-year-old adult, the prescription reads ibuprofen 800 mg every 4 hours as needed, and the maximum dose of ibuprofen is 3.2 g/day.</li><li style="margin:4px 0"><b>A:</b> Taken every 4 hours for 24 hours, that is 6 doses, or 4.8 g, so the total daily dose prescribed is too high.</li><li style="margin:4px 0"><b>R:</b> I recommend a new prescription with directions that stay at or under 3.2 g/day, such as 800 mg every 8 hours as needed (2.4 g/day).</li></ul><p style="margin:6px 0;font-size:0.85em;color:var(--text-dim)">Basis: Maximum 3.2 g/day: Week 11 deck, Background example. EOOR key, station 7: contact prescriber with new recommendation. The arithmetic and the regimen in the recommendation are computed for practice.</p></div></details></div>
<div style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:8px;background:var(--paper);color:var(--text);padding:10px 12px;margin:10px 0;box-sizing:border-box;max-width:100%;overflow-wrap:anywhere"><b>3. Incorrect strength prescribed</b><p style="margin:4px 0;color:var(--text)"><i>Case:</i> Levothyroxine 25 mg tablets, one tablet daily.</p><details style="border-top:1px solid var(--line);margin:6px 0 0;padding:0"><summary style="min-height:40px;display:flex;align-items:center;cursor:pointer;font-weight:600;padding:4px 0;box-sizing:border-box">Try it: write the four parts, then open this</summary><div style="padding:4px 0 8px"><ul style="list-style:none;margin:4px 0;padding:0;color:var(--text)"><li style="margin:4px 0"><b>S:</b> I am calling about a levothyroxine prescription you sent to our pharmacy today.</li><li style="margin:4px 0"><b>B:</b> The patient is a 60-year-old adult, the prescription reads levothyroxine 25 mg, and levothyroxine is dosed in micrograms, so 25 mg equals 25,000 mcg.</li><li style="margin:4px 0"><b>A:</b> The strength prescribed is incorrect: 25 mg is 1,000 times 25 mcg, and a dose that large could harm the patient.</li><li style="margin:4px 0"><b>R:</b> I recommend a new prescription for levothyroxine 25 mcg, one tablet daily, if that is the strength you intended.</li></ul><p style="margin:6px 0;font-size:0.85em;color:var(--text-dim)">Basis: Week 11 deck, types of prescribing errors: levothyroxine 25 mg instead of 25 mcg. 1 mg = 1,000 mcg. The age and the daily sig are invented for practice.</p></div></details></div>
<div style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:8px;background:var(--paper);color:var(--text);padding:10px 12px;margin:10px 0;box-sizing:border-box;max-width:100%;overflow-wrap:anywhere"><b>4. Incorrect frequency for the formulation</b><p style="margin:4px 0;color:var(--text)"><i>Case:</i> Focalin XR 10 mg, one capsule twice daily.</p><details style="border-top:1px solid var(--line);margin:6px 0 0;padding:0"><summary style="min-height:40px;display:flex;align-items:center;cursor:pointer;font-weight:600;padding:4px 0;box-sizing:border-box">Try it: write the four parts, then open this</summary><div style="padding:4px 0 8px"><ul style="list-style:none;margin:4px 0;padding:0;color:var(--text)"><li style="margin:4px 0"><b>S:</b> I am calling to clarify the directions on a Focalin XR prescription we received for your patient.</li><li style="margin:4px 0"><b>B:</b> The patient is a 24-year-old adult; the prescription is Focalin XR 10 mg, one capsule twice daily, and Focalin XR is an extended-release product given once daily in the morning.</li><li style="margin:4px 0"><b>A:</b> Twice-daily dosing of an extended-release product means the frequency prescribed is too high, and the patient would take two extended-release doses a day.</li><li style="margin:4px 0"><b>R:</b> I recommend a new prescription for Focalin XR 10 mg, one capsule once daily in the morning.</li></ul><p style="margin:6px 0;font-size:0.85em;color:var(--text-dim)">Basis: Week 11 deck: XR/ER product prescribed multiple times daily is an incorrect frequency for the formulation. Intro to Dispensing slide 23: example Focalin XR 10 mg, one capsule daily in the morning. The age is invented for practice.</p></div></details></div>
<div style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:8px;background:var(--paper);color:var(--text);padding:10px 12px;margin:10px 0;box-sizing:border-box;max-width:100%;overflow-wrap:anywhere"><b>5. Insufficient prescribed quantity (liquids)</b><p style="margin:4px 0;color:var(--text)"><i>Case:</i> Sulfamethoxazole/trimethoprim suspension, 7.5 mL twice daily for 5 days for shigellosis, quantity 60 mL.</p><details style="border-top:1px solid var(--line);margin:6px 0 0;padding:0"><summary style="min-height:40px;display:flex;align-items:center;cursor:pointer;font-weight:600;padding:4px 0;box-sizing:border-box">Try it: write the four parts, then open this</summary><div style="padding:4px 0 8px"><ul style="list-style:none;margin:4px 0;padding:0;color:var(--text)"><li style="margin:4px 0"><b>S:</b> I am calling about the quantity on a sulfamethoxazole/trimethoprim suspension prescription you sent today.</li><li style="margin:4px 0"><b>B:</b> The patient is an 3-year-old child, and the directions are 7.5 mL twice daily for 5 days for shigellosis, which needs 75 mL, while 60 mL was prescribed.</li><li style="margin:4px 0"><b>A:</b> The quantity prescribed is insufficient: 60 mL covers only 4 days, so the patient would run out before the course ends.</li><li style="margin:4px 0"><b>R:</b> I recommend a new prescription for 75 mL so that the full 5-day course can be dispensed.</li></ul><p style="margin:6px 0;font-size:0.85em;color:var(--text-dim)">Basis: Week 11 deck: insufficient prescribed quantity, common with liquids. EOOR key, station 9, uses 60 mL versus 75 mL as a dispensing error (less filled than prescribed); here the prescription itself says 60 mL, so it is a prescribing error. The child’s age and the sig are invented case detail for practice (a pediatric-size dose); 7.5 mL × 2 × 5 = 75 mL.</p></div></details></div>
<div style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:8px;background:var(--paper);color:var(--text);padding:10px 12px;margin:10px 0;box-sizing:border-box;max-width:100%;overflow-wrap:anywhere"><b>6. Inappropriate number of refills</b><p style="margin:4px 0;color:var(--text)"><i>Case:</i> Focalin XR 10 mg, 30 capsules, one every morning, 2 refills.</p><details style="border-top:1px solid var(--line);margin:6px 0 0;padding:0"><summary style="min-height:40px;display:flex;align-items:center;cursor:pointer;font-weight:600;padding:4px 0;box-sizing:border-box">Try it: write the four parts, then open this</summary><div style="padding:4px 0 8px"><ul style="list-style:none;margin:4px 0;padding:0;color:var(--text)"><li style="margin:4px 0"><b>S:</b> I am calling about a Focalin XR prescription you sent to our pharmacy today.</li><li style="margin:4px 0"><b>B:</b> The patient is a 24-year-old adult, and Focalin XR 10 mg was prescribed with 2 refills; Focalin XR is a schedule II drug, and schedule II prescriptions are allowed no refills.</li><li style="margin:4px 0"><b>A:</b> Two refills were prescribed on this schedule II prescription, so the number of refills is inappropriate.</li><li style="margin:4px 0"><b>R:</b> I recommend a new prescription for Focalin XR 10 mg, 30 capsules, with no refills.</li></ul><p style="margin:6px 0;font-size:0.85em;color:var(--text-dim)">Basis: Intro to Dispensing slides 22 and 27: schedule II 0 refills; schedule III–V up to 5 in 6 months. EOOR key, station 13: CII cannot have refills, call provider. Focalin XR is shown under the Schedule II heading on Intro to Dispensing slide 23.</p></div></details></div>
<div style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:8px;background:var(--paper);color:var(--text);padding:10px 12px;margin:10px 0;box-sizing:border-box;max-width:100%;overflow-wrap:anywhere"><b>7. DEA number required but missing (CIII–V)</b><p style="margin:4px 0;color:var(--text)"><i>Case:</i> Tylenol with Codeine #3, no DEA number on the prescription.</p><details style="border-top:1px solid var(--line);margin:6px 0 0;padding:0"><summary style="min-height:40px;display:flex;align-items:center;cursor:pointer;font-weight:600;padding:4px 0;box-sizing:border-box">Try it: write the four parts, then open this</summary><div style="padding:4px 0 8px"><ul style="list-style:none;margin:4px 0;padding:0;color:var(--text)"><li style="margin:4px 0"><b>S:</b> I am calling about a Tylenol with Codeine #3 prescription you sent for your patient today.</li><li style="margin:4px 0"><b>B:</b> The patient is a 52-year-old adult, Tylenol with Codeine #3 is a schedule III controlled substance, and a CIII–V prescription must include the prescriber’s DEA number.</li><li style="margin:4px 0"><b>A:</b> The DEA number is missing, so the prescription is incomplete and cannot be filled until it is supplied.</li><li style="margin:4px 0"><b>R:</b> I recommend that you provide your DEA number for this prescription so that we can complete the review and fill it.</li></ul><p style="margin:6px 0;font-size:0.85em;color:var(--text-dim)">Basis: Intro to Dispensing slide 18: DEA number required on CIII–V. EOOR key, stations 6 and 13: contact prescriber for DEA number. Acetaminophen/codeine is CS-III in the Basic Drug Knowledge list. The age is invented for practice.</p></div></details></div>
<p style="margin:6px 0 14px;font-size:0.82em;color:var(--text-dim)">Source: APC-III-Week-11-OSCE-Prep_2026.pdf (types of prescribing errors; Background vs Assessment); Intro-to-Dispensing.pdf slides 18–23, 27, 38–39; EOOR-Answer-Key.pdf stations 6, 7, 9 and 13; 2025-2026 Basic Drug Knowledge List.pdf (CS schedules). Conflict: CII expiration is 30 days on slide 22 and more than 1 month in the EOOR key; no model here depends on it.</p>
<h4 style="margin:18px 0 6px;font-size:1.05em;color:var(--text)">2. Full case drill: six prescriptions</h4>
<p style="margin:6px 0;color:var(--text)">For each case, read the prescription and the label, find the error, then write the SBAR out loud or on paper before you open the model. In every case the label and the stock bottle match the prescription.</p>
<div style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:8px;background:var(--paper);color:var(--text);padding:10px 12px;margin:10px 0;box-sizing:border-box;max-width:100%;overflow-wrap:anywhere"><b>Case 1: pain prescription</b><div style="border:1px dashed var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 10px;margin:6px 0;background:var(--paper);color:var(--text);box-sizing:border-box;max-width:100%;overflow-wrap:anywhere">San Antonio Health Clinic · Date: 11/19/2026<br>Patient: Sample Patient A, 46 y<br>Ibuprofen 800 mg tablets<br>QTY: 90 tablets<br>SIG: Take 1 tablet PO every 4 hours as needed for pain<br>Refills: 0 · Dr. Sample · Substitution permitted<br>Label: ibuprofen 800 mg, take 1 tablet by mouth every 4 hours as needed for pain, qty 90</div><p style="margin:6px 0;font-weight:600;color:var(--text)">Step 1: find the error and its type (prescribing, transcribing or dispensing) and who fixes it.</p><details style="border-top:1px solid var(--line);margin:6px 0 0;padding:0"><summary style="min-height:40px;display:flex;align-items:center;cursor:pointer;font-weight:600;padding:4px 0;box-sizing:border-box">Reveal Step 1</summary><div style="padding:4px 0 8px"><p style="margin:6px 0;color:var(--text)">Prescribing error: prescribed dose too high (6 doses of 800 mg is 4.8 g in 24 hours; the maximum is 3.2 g/day). The label matches the prescription, so the fix is a call to the prescriber for a new recommendation (EOOR key, station 7: contact prescriber with new recommendation).</p></div></details><p style="margin:6px 0;font-weight:600;color:var(--text)">Step 2: write your SBAR out loud or on paper.</p><details style="border-top:1px solid var(--line);margin:6px 0 0;padding:0"><summary style="min-height:40px;display:flex;align-items:center;cursor:pointer;font-weight:600;padding:4px 0;box-sizing:border-box">Reveal the model SBAR and the checklist</summary><div style="padding:4px 0 8px"><ul style="list-style:none;margin:4px 0;padding:0;color:var(--text)"><li style="margin:4px 0"><b>S:</b> I am calling about an ibuprofen prescription you sent to our pharmacy on 11/19.</li><li style="margin:4px 0"><b>B:</b> Sample Patient A is 46 years old, the prescription reads ibuprofen 800 mg every 4 hours as needed, and the maximum dose of ibuprofen is 3.2 g/day.</li><li style="margin:4px 0"><b>A:</b> Taken every 4 hours for 24 hours, that is 6 doses, or 4.8 g, so the total daily dose prescribed is too high.</li><li style="margin:4px 0"><b>R:</b> I recommend a new prescription with directions that stay at or under 3.2 g/day, such as 800 mg every 8 hours as needed.</li></ul><p style="margin:6px 0 2px;color:var(--text)"><b>Checklist: your SBAR should have</b></p><ul style="margin:6px 0 6px 18px;padding:0;color:var(--text)"><li style="margin:3px 0">Names the patient and the drug</li><li style="margin:3px 0">Gives one clinical fact as Background (not the problem)</li><li style="margin:3px 0">States the problem as the Assessment, with why it matters</li><li style="margin:3px 0">Makes a specific Recommendation to the prescriber (not a vague request, and not a change you make alone)</li><li style="margin:3px 0">Follows the order Situation, Background, Assessment, Recommendation</li><li style="margin:3px 0">Is concise</li></ul></div></details></div>
<div style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:8px;background:var(--paper);color:var(--text);padding:10px 12px;margin:10px 0;box-sizing:border-box;max-width:100%;overflow-wrap:anywhere"><b>Case 2: anxiety prescription (written in April)</b><div style="border:1px dashed var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 10px;margin:6px 0;background:var(--paper);color:var(--text);box-sizing:border-box;max-width:100%;overflow-wrap:anywhere">San Antonio Health Clinic · Date: 4/6/2026 · Presented 11/21/2026<br>Patient: Sample Patient B, 46 y · DOB and address on prescription<br>Clonazepam 0.5 mg tablets<br>QTY: 30 (thirty) tablets<br>SIG: Take 1 tablet PO twice daily as needed for anxiety<br>Refills: 0 · DEA number present · Dr. Sample<br>Label: clonazepam 0.5 mg, take 1 tablet by mouth twice daily as needed for anxiety, qty 30</div><p style="margin:6px 0;font-weight:600;color:var(--text)">Step 1: find the error and its type (prescribing, transcribing or dispensing) and who fixes it.</p><details style="border-top:1px solid var(--line);margin:6px 0 0;padding:0"><summary style="min-height:40px;display:flex;align-items:center;cursor:pointer;font-weight:600;padding:4px 0;box-sizing:border-box">Reveal Step 1</summary><div style="padding:4px 0 8px"><p style="margin:6px 0;color:var(--text)">Prescribing error: prescription is expired. Schedule III–V prescriptions expire 6 months after issue, so 6 months after 4/6/2026 is 10/6/2026, and 11/21/2026 is 46 days past that date, which means it has expired. Fix: contact the prescriber for a new prescription (EOOR key, station 6).</p></div></details><p style="margin:6px 0;font-weight:600;color:var(--text)">Step 2: write your SBAR out loud or on paper.</p><details style="border-top:1px solid var(--line);margin:6px 0 0;padding:0"><summary style="min-height:40px;display:flex;align-items:center;cursor:pointer;font-weight:600;padding:4px 0;box-sizing:border-box">Reveal the model SBAR and the checklist</summary><div style="padding:4px 0 8px"><ul style="list-style:none;margin:4px 0;padding:0;color:var(--text)"><li style="margin:4px 0"><b>S:</b> I am calling about a clonazepam prescription for your patient.</li><li style="margin:4px 0"><b>B:</b> Sample Patient B is 46 years old; the prescription for clonazepam 0.5 mg was written on 4/6/2026, and a CIII–V prescription expires 6 months after it is issued.</li><li style="margin:4px 0"><b>A:</b> Six months after 4/6/2026 is 10/6/2026, and 11/21/2026 is 46 days past that date, so the prescription has expired and cannot be filled.</li><li style="margin:4px 0"><b>R:</b> I recommend that you send a new prescription for clonazepam 0.5 mg.</li></ul><p style="margin:6px 0 2px;color:var(--text)"><b>Checklist: your SBAR should have</b></p><ul style="margin:6px 0 6px 18px;padding:0;color:var(--text)"><li style="margin:3px 0">Names the patient and the drug</li><li style="margin:3px 0">Gives one clinical fact as Background (not the problem)</li><li style="margin:3px 0">States the problem as the Assessment, with why it matters</li><li style="margin:3px 0">Makes a specific Recommendation to the prescriber (not a vague request, and not a change you make alone)</li><li style="margin:3px 0">Follows the order Situation, Background, Assessment, Recommendation</li><li style="margin:3px 0">Is concise</li></ul></div></details></div>
<div style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:8px;background:var(--paper);color:var(--text);padding:10px 12px;margin:10px 0;box-sizing:border-box;max-width:100%;overflow-wrap:anywhere"><b>Case 3: pain prescription (controlled)</b><div style="border:1px dashed var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 10px;margin:6px 0;background:var(--paper);color:var(--text);box-sizing:border-box;max-width:100%;overflow-wrap:anywhere">San Antonio Health Clinic · Date: 11/18/2026<br>Patient: Sample Patient C, 52 y · DOB and address on prescription<br>Tylenol with Codeine #3 tablets<br>QTY: 20 (twenty) tablets<br>SIG: Take 1 tablet PO every 6 hours as needed for pain<br>Refills: 0 · Dr. Sample · DEA number: not written<br>Label: Tylenol with Codeine #3, take 1 tablet by mouth every 6 hours as needed for pain, qty 20</div><p style="margin:6px 0;font-weight:600;color:var(--text)">Step 1: find the error and its type (prescribing, transcribing or dispensing) and who fixes it.</p><details style="border-top:1px solid var(--line);margin:6px 0 0;padding:0"><summary style="min-height:40px;display:flex;align-items:center;cursor:pointer;font-weight:600;padding:4px 0;box-sizing:border-box">Reveal Step 1</summary><div style="padding:4px 0 8px"><p style="margin:6px 0;color:var(--text)">Prescribing error: DEA number required but missing (CIII–V). Fix: contact the prescriber for the DEA number (EOOR key, stations 6 and 13).</p></div></details><p style="margin:6px 0;font-weight:600;color:var(--text)">Step 2: write your SBAR out loud or on paper.</p><details style="border-top:1px solid var(--line);margin:6px 0 0;padding:0"><summary style="min-height:40px;display:flex;align-items:center;cursor:pointer;font-weight:600;padding:4px 0;box-sizing:border-box">Reveal the model SBAR and the checklist</summary><div style="padding:4px 0 8px"><ul style="list-style:none;margin:4px 0;padding:0;color:var(--text)"><li style="margin:4px 0"><b>S:</b> I am calling about a Tylenol with Codeine #3 prescription you sent for your patient on 11/18.</li><li style="margin:4px 0"><b>B:</b> Sample Patient C is 52 years old, Tylenol with Codeine #3 is a schedule III controlled substance, and a CIII–V prescription must include the prescriber’s DEA number.</li><li style="margin:4px 0"><b>A:</b> The DEA number is missing, so the prescription is incomplete and cannot be filled until it is supplied.</li><li style="margin:4px 0"><b>R:</b> I recommend that you provide your DEA number for this prescription so that we can complete the review and fill it.</li></ul><p style="margin:6px 0 2px;color:var(--text)"><b>Checklist: your SBAR should have</b></p><ul style="margin:6px 0 6px 18px;padding:0;color:var(--text)"><li style="margin:3px 0">Names the patient and the drug</li><li style="margin:3px 0">Gives one clinical fact as Background (not the problem)</li><li style="margin:3px 0">States the problem as the Assessment, with why it matters</li><li style="margin:3px 0">Makes a specific Recommendation to the prescriber (not a vague request, and not a change you make alone)</li><li style="margin:3px 0">Follows the order Situation, Background, Assessment, Recommendation</li><li style="margin:3px 0">Is concise</li></ul></div></details></div>
<div style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:8px;background:var(--paper);color:var(--text);padding:10px 12px;margin:10px 0;box-sizing:border-box;max-width:100%;overflow-wrap:anywhere"><b>Case 4: stimulant prescription</b><div style="border:1px dashed var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 10px;margin:6px 0;background:var(--paper);color:var(--text);box-sizing:border-box;max-width:100%;overflow-wrap:anywhere">San Antonio Health Clinic · Date: 11/19/2026<br>Patient: Sample Patient D, 24 y · DOB and address on prescription<br>Focalin XR 10 mg capsules<br>QTY: 30 (thirty) capsules<br>SIG: Take 1 capsule PO daily in the morning<br>Refills: 2 · DEA number present · Dr. Sample<br>Label: Focalin XR 10 mg, take 1 capsule by mouth daily in the morning, qty 30, refills 2</div><p style="margin:6px 0;font-weight:600;color:var(--text)">Step 1: find the error and its type (prescribing, transcribing or dispensing) and who fixes it.</p><details style="border-top:1px solid var(--line);margin:6px 0 0;padding:0"><summary style="min-height:40px;display:flex;align-items:center;cursor:pointer;font-weight:600;padding:4px 0;box-sizing:border-box">Reveal Step 1</summary><div style="padding:4px 0 8px"><p style="margin:6px 0;color:var(--text)">Prescribing error: inappropriate number of refills. Focalin XR is schedule II, which is allowed no refills (Intro to Dispensing slides 22 and 27). Fix: call the provider for a prescription without refills (EOOR key, station 13).</p></div></details><p style="margin:6px 0;font-weight:600;color:var(--text)">Step 2: write your SBAR out loud or on paper.</p><details style="border-top:1px solid var(--line);margin:6px 0 0;padding:0"><summary style="min-height:40px;display:flex;align-items:center;cursor:pointer;font-weight:600;padding:4px 0;box-sizing:border-box">Reveal the model SBAR and the checklist</summary><div style="padding:4px 0 8px"><ul style="list-style:none;margin:4px 0;padding:0;color:var(--text)"><li style="margin:4px 0"><b>S:</b> I am calling about a Focalin XR prescription you sent to our pharmacy on 11/19.</li><li style="margin:4px 0"><b>B:</b> Sample Patient D is 24 years old, and Focalin XR 10 mg was prescribed with 2 refills; Focalin XR is a schedule II drug, and schedule II prescriptions are allowed no refills.</li><li style="margin:4px 0"><b>A:</b> Two refills were prescribed on this schedule II prescription, so the number of refills is inappropriate.</li><li style="margin:4px 0"><b>R:</b> I recommend a new prescription for Focalin XR 10 mg, 30 capsules, with no refills.</li></ul><p style="margin:6px 0 2px;color:var(--text)"><b>Checklist: your SBAR should have</b></p><ul style="margin:6px 0 6px 18px;padding:0;color:var(--text)"><li style="margin:3px 0">Names the patient and the drug</li><li style="margin:3px 0">Gives one clinical fact as Background (not the problem)</li><li style="margin:3px 0">States the problem as the Assessment, with why it matters</li><li style="margin:3px 0">Makes a specific Recommendation to the prescriber (not a vague request, and not a change you make alone)</li><li style="margin:3px 0">Follows the order Situation, Background, Assessment, Recommendation</li><li style="margin:3px 0">Is concise</li></ul></div></details></div>
<div style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:8px;background:var(--paper);color:var(--text);padding:10px 12px;margin:10px 0;box-sizing:border-box;max-width:100%;overflow-wrap:anywhere"><b>Case 5: blood pressure prescription (written last year)</b><div style="border:1px dashed var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 10px;margin:6px 0;background:var(--paper);color:var(--text);box-sizing:border-box;max-width:100%;overflow-wrap:anywhere">San Antonio Health Clinic · Date: 9/15/2025 · Presented 11/21/2026<br>Patient: Sample Patient E · address in the pharmacy record<br>Lisinopril 20 mg tablets<br>QTY: 30 tablets<br>SIG: Take 1 tablet PO daily<br>Refills: 6 · Indication: hypertension · Dr. Sample<br>Label: lisinopril 20 mg, take 1 tablet by mouth daily, qty 30</div><p style="margin:6px 0;font-weight:600;color:var(--text)">Step 1: find the error and its type (prescribing, transcribing or dispensing) and who fixes it.</p><details style="border-top:1px solid var(--line);margin:6px 0 0;padding:0"><summary style="min-height:40px;display:flex;align-items:center;cursor:pointer;font-weight:600;padding:4px 0;box-sizing:border-box">Reveal Step 1</summary><div style="padding:4px 0 8px"><p style="margin:6px 0;color:var(--text)">Prescribing error: prescription is expired. Lisinopril is a dangerous (non-controlled) drug, and these prescriptions expire 1 year after issue (slide 22), so 1 year after 9/15/2025 is 9/15/2026, and 11/21/2026 is 67 days past that date, which means it has expired (EOOR key, station 8: more than 12 months, call prescriber for a new prescription).</p></div></details><p style="margin:6px 0;font-weight:600;color:var(--text)">Step 2: write your SBAR out loud or on paper.</p><details style="border-top:1px solid var(--line);margin:6px 0 0;padding:0"><summary style="min-height:40px;display:flex;align-items:center;cursor:pointer;font-weight:600;padding:4px 0;box-sizing:border-box">Reveal the model SBAR and the checklist</summary><div style="padding:4px 0 8px"><ul style="list-style:none;margin:4px 0;padding:0;color:var(--text)"><li style="margin:4px 0"><b>S:</b> I am calling about a lisinopril prescription for your patient.</li><li style="margin:4px 0"><b>B:</b> Sample Patient E has a lisinopril 20 mg prescription written on 9/15/2025, and a prescription for a dangerous drug expires 1 year after it is issued.</li><li style="margin:4px 0"><b>A:</b> One year after 9/15/2025 is 9/15/2026, and 11/21/2026 is 67 days past that date, so the prescription has expired and cannot be filled.</li><li style="margin:4px 0"><b>R:</b> I recommend that you send a new prescription for lisinopril 20 mg.</li></ul><p style="margin:6px 0 2px;color:var(--text)"><b>Checklist: your SBAR should have</b></p><ul style="margin:6px 0 6px 18px;padding:0;color:var(--text)"><li style="margin:3px 0">Names the patient and the drug</li><li style="margin:3px 0">Gives one clinical fact as Background (not the problem)</li><li style="margin:3px 0">States the problem as the Assessment, with why it matters</li><li style="margin:3px 0">Makes a specific Recommendation to the prescriber (not a vague request, and not a change you make alone)</li><li style="margin:3px 0">Follows the order Situation, Background, Assessment, Recommendation</li><li style="margin:3px 0">Is concise</li></ul></div></details></div>
<div style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:8px;background:var(--paper);color:var(--text);padding:10px 12px;margin:10px 0;box-sizing:border-box;max-width:100%;overflow-wrap:anywhere"><b>Case 6: stimulant prescription, second version</b><div style="border:1px dashed var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 10px;margin:6px 0;background:var(--paper);color:var(--text);box-sizing:border-box;max-width:100%;overflow-wrap:anywhere">San Antonio Health Clinic · Date: 11/20/2026<br>Patient: Sample Patient F, 24 y · DOB and address on prescription<br>Focalin XR 10 mg capsules<br>QTY: 60 (sixty) capsules<br>SIG: Take 1 capsule PO twice daily<br>Refills: 0 · DEA number present · Dr. Sample<br>Label: Focalin XR 10 mg, take 1 capsule by mouth twice daily, qty 60</div><p style="margin:6px 0;font-weight:600;color:var(--text)">Step 1: find the error and its type (prescribing, transcribing or dispensing) and who fixes it.</p><details style="border-top:1px solid var(--line);margin:6px 0 0;padding:0"><summary style="min-height:40px;display:flex;align-items:center;cursor:pointer;font-weight:600;padding:4px 0;box-sizing:border-box">Reveal Step 1</summary><div style="padding:4px 0 8px"><p style="margin:6px 0;color:var(--text)">Prescribing error: incorrect frequency for the formulation (an XR product prescribed twice daily). Fix: call the prescriber for a corrected prescription. The quantity of 60 matches the twice-daily sig, so the quantity is not the error.</p></div></details><p style="margin:6px 0;font-weight:600;color:var(--text)">Step 2: write your SBAR out loud or on paper.</p><details style="border-top:1px solid var(--line);margin:6px 0 0;padding:0"><summary style="min-height:40px;display:flex;align-items:center;cursor:pointer;font-weight:600;padding:4px 0;box-sizing:border-box">Reveal the model SBAR and the checklist</summary><div style="padding:4px 0 8px"><ul style="list-style:none;margin:4px 0;padding:0;color:var(--text)"><li style="margin:4px 0"><b>S:</b> I am calling to clarify the directions on a Focalin XR prescription we received for your patient on 11/20.</li><li style="margin:4px 0"><b>B:</b> Sample Patient F is 24 years old; the prescription is Focalin XR 10 mg, one capsule twice daily, and Focalin XR is an extended-release product given once daily in the morning.</li><li style="margin:4px 0"><b>A:</b> Twice-daily dosing of an extended-release product means the frequency prescribed is too high, and the patient would take two extended-release doses a day.</li><li style="margin:4px 0"><b>R:</b> I recommend a new prescription for Focalin XR 10 mg, one capsule once daily in the morning.</li></ul><p style="margin:6px 0 2px;color:var(--text)"><b>Checklist: your SBAR should have</b></p><ul style="margin:6px 0 6px 18px;padding:0;color:var(--text)"><li style="margin:3px 0">Names the patient and the drug</li><li style="margin:3px 0">Gives one clinical fact as Background (not the problem)</li><li style="margin:3px 0">States the problem as the Assessment, with why it matters</li><li style="margin:3px 0">Makes a specific Recommendation to the prescriber (not a vague request, and not a change you make alone)</li><li style="margin:3px 0">Follows the order Situation, Background, Assessment, Recommendation</li><li style="margin:3px 0">Is concise</li></ul></div></details></div>
<p style="margin:6px 0 14px;font-size:0.82em;color:var(--text-dim)">Source: EOOR-Answer-Key.pdf stations 6, 7, 8 and 13; Intro-to-Dispensing.pdf slides 18–23, 27, 38–39; APC-III-Week-11-OSCE-Prep_2026.pdf. Patients, ages, prescriber, clinic dates and quantities are invented for practice; patient names are placeholders.</p>
<h4 style="margin:18px 0 6px;font-size:1.05em;color:var(--text)">3. Immunization SBAR case</h4>
<p style="margin:6px 0;color:var(--text)">Week 11: the immunization station involves a patient who is pregnant or immunocompromised. Use Table 2 of the AAFP 2026 schedule (columns Pregnancy and Immunocompromised), read the legend printed under the table, then read the Notes for each vaccine you recommend.</p>
<div style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:8px;background:var(--paper);color:var(--text);padding:10px 12px;margin:10px 0;box-sizing:border-box;max-width:100%;overflow-wrap:anywhere"><b>Case: pregnant patient, 11/21/2026</b><p style="margin:4px 0;color:var(--text)">A 28-year-old patient is 33 weeks pregnant today, has received no vaccines this pregnancy, and no vaccine allergies are reported. You are the intern writing the SBAR for the preceptor.</p><details style="border-top:1px solid var(--line);margin:6px 0 0;padding:0"><summary style="min-height:40px;display:flex;align-items:center;cursor:pointer;font-weight:600;padding:4px 0;box-sizing:border-box">Reveal the model SBAR and the checklist</summary><div style="padding:4px 0 8px"><ul style="list-style:none;margin:4px 0;padding:0;color:var(--text)"><li style="margin:4px 0"><b>S:</b> A patient is at the pharmacy to be assessed for vaccine needs.</li><li style="margin:4px 0"><b>B:</b> She is 28 years old and 33 weeks pregnant today, has received no vaccines this pregnancy, and has no vaccine allergies reported.</li><li style="margin:4px 0"><b>A:</b> AAFP Table 2 and Notes recommend inactivated influenza, COVID-19 and Tdap (within weeks 27–36, early part preferred) in pregnancy, and Abrysvo at 32 weeks 0 days through 36 weeks 6 days from September through January; LAIV3, MMR and varicella are contraindicated.</li><li style="margin:4px 0"><b>R:</b> I recommend an inactivated influenza vaccine, a COVID-19 vaccine, Tdap and Abrysvo today.</li></ul><p style="margin:6px 0 2px;color:var(--text)"><b>Checklist: read the colors in the Pregnancy column of Table 2</b></p><ul style="margin:6px 0 6px 18px;padding:0;color:var(--text)"><li style="margin:3px 0"><b>Yellow</b> (recommended for all adults who lack documentation): influenza inactivated or recombinant, COVID-19. RSV is yellow with “Seasonal administration, See Notes”: the Notes give Abrysvo at 32 weeks 0 days through 36 weeks 6 days, September through January. At 33 weeks on 11/21 both conditions are met.</li><li style="margin:3px 0"><b>Orange</b> (recommended for all adults; additional doses may be needed): Tdap, 1 dose each pregnancy, preferably in the early part of weeks 27–36.</li><li style="margin:3px 0"><b>Red</b> (contraindicated or not recommended): LAIV3 (FluMist), MMR, varicella and HPV. An asterisk means vaccinate after pregnancy, if indicated.</li><li style="margin:3px 0"><b>Navy</b> (precaution): MenB and IPV; the Notes say to delay MenB until after pregnancy unless the patient is at increased risk and the benefit outweighs the risk.</li><li style="margin:3px 0">Situation is one simple sentence; Background has the patient characteristics and vaccine history; Assessment names the AAFP as the source and says what it recommends; Recommendation lists the vaccines.</li><li style="margin:3px 0">Variation: if the patient were 30 weeks 0 days on 11/21/2026, Abrysvo would not be due until 32 weeks 0 days, which is 12/5/2026, 14 days later.</li></ul><p style="margin:6px 0;color:var(--text)"><b>Immunocompromised variation</b> (lupus on an immunosuppressant, asks for FluMist): in the Immunocompromised column LAIV3 is red, so the Assessment states the contraindication (the Appendix lists immunocompromise from any cause, including medications and HIV infection) and the Recommendation gives an inactivated influenza vaccine, which is yellow, 1 dose annually. RZV is yellow with See Notes: 2 doses 2–6 months apart for immunocompromising conditions. Pneumococcal is yellow.</p><p style="margin:6px 0;color:var(--text)"><b>HIV variation:</b> MMR is red when the CD4 percentage is below 15% or the count is below 200 cells/mm3; at 15% and 200 or above for at least 6 months with no evidence of immunity the Notes give 2 doses at least 4 weeks apart.</p></div></details></div>
<p style="margin:6px 0 14px;font-size:0.82em;color:var(--text-dim)">Source: Immunizations-Pre-Lab.pdf slides 20–22; 9/21 transcript (T: Background is the characteristics that back up the assessment; situation should be simple; cite the source); AAFP-Adult-Schedule-2026.pdf Table 2 (page 3), Notes (RSV, Tdap, RZV, MMR, MenB) and Appendix (pages 9–10); APC-III-Week-11-OSCE-Prep_2026.pdf (immunization station). Age and gestational week are invented for practice.</p>
<h4 style="margin:18px 0 6px;font-size:1.05em;color:var(--text)">4. Two SBAR versions</h4>
<div style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:8px;background:var(--paper);color:var(--text);padding:10px 12px;margin:10px 0;box-sizing:border-box;max-width:100%;overflow-wrap:anywhere"><b>Prescription-error SBAR (Week 11 deck)</b><ul style="margin:6px 0 6px 18px;padding:0;color:var(--text)"><li style="margin:3px 0">Goes to the prescriber after a prescribing error.</li><li style="margin:3px 0">Background = who the patient is, the prescription in question and the clinical facts (for example, the maximum ibuprofen dose is 3.2 g/day).</li><li style="margin:3px 0">Assessment = the actual problem and why it matters (for example, the total daily dose prescribed is too high).</li><li style="margin:3px 0">Recommendation = the correction you ask the prescriber to make.</li></ul></div>
<div style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:8px;background:var(--paper);color:var(--text);padding:10px 12px;margin:10px 0;box-sizing:border-box;max-width:100%;overflow-wrap:anywhere"><b>Vaccine-needs SBAR (Immunizations slide 22)</b><ul style="margin:6px 0 6px 18px;padding:0;color:var(--text)"><li style="margin:3px 0">Goes to the preceptor or prescriber with an immunization recommendation.</li><li style="margin:3px 0">Background = patient characteristics and vaccine history (for example, 61 years old, health care worker, type 2 diabetes, vaccine history).</li><li style="margin:3px 0">Assessment = what the AAFP schedule recommends for that patient, with the source named (for example, AAFP recommends PCV21 for ages 19–64 with diabetes).</li><li style="margin:3px 0">Recommendation = the vaccines you recommend, including the next dose (for example, a second Shingrix dose in 2–6 months).</li></ul></div>
<p style="margin:6px 0;color:var(--text)">Test: a sentence that reports something known about the patient or the drug is Background in both versions. In the prescription-error SBAR the rule or limit is Background and the violation is Assessment; in the vaccine-needs SBAR the AAFP recommendation for this patient is Assessment. What changes is the kind of conclusion: in a prescription error it is the problem; in a vaccine SBAR it is what the schedule recommends.</p>
<p style="margin:6px 0 14px;font-size:0.82em;color:var(--text-dim)">Source: APC-III-Week-11-OSCE-Prep_2026.pdf (Background vs Assessment); Immunizations-Pre-Lab.pdf slides 20–22; 9/21 transcript (T).</p>
</div></details>
`;
